{-
   `@allocator(name)` - Design-Allocator.md §18.3's provider selection.

   The second clause of the filter `@platform` is the first of, on a *policy* axis rather than a
   machine one. What it has to do, and what a pick between claims could not, is **delete** the
   providers it did not select: a declaration this build did not choose is not read at all, so an
   unfinished provider is not a file every program has to compile.

   That is what this fixture asserts, and it asserts it the only way the property is visible from
   source - by declaring the same name twice under different providers, which is a duplicate
   declaration in any build that read both.
-}

@allocator(bootstrap) fn provided() -> Int = 10
@allocator(concurrent) fn provided() -> Int = 20
@allocator(arena) fn provided() -> Int = 30

-- A record, to show that selection is not a rule about functions: it is a declaration filter, and
-- every kind of declaration goes through it.
@allocator(bootstrap) data Chosen {v: Int}
@allocator(concurrent) data Chosen {v: Int, extra: Int}

-- And an unselected declaration is *absent* rather than shadowed, which is what makes a name it
-- alone declares unresolvable. Nothing here can observe that directly - the fixture would not
-- compile - so it is stated rather than tested. `Reject.Allocator.yana` is the other half of the
-- attribute: what it refuses, and the name it deliberately does not.
@allocator(bootstrap) fn onlyInBootstrap() -> Int = 5

fn main() -> Int = provided() + Chosen {v: 1}.v + onlyInBootstrap()
